1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,235
In 1954, subscribers to the Great Soviet
Encyclopedia received a letter from the publisher.

2
00:00:07,235 --> 00:00:12,800
It came with replacement pages, and
instructions to use a razor blade.

3
00:00:13,150 --> 00:00:21,750
Volume two contained a three-page article on Lavrentiy
Beria. Beria had been shot. The pages had to go.

4
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:28,867
What arrived to fill the gap was an expanded
entry on the Bering Sea. History was corrected.

5
00:00:28,867 --> 00:00:32,250
The binding stayed exactly as thick as before.

6
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:40,925
That gets told as a story about the Soviet Union. It
is really a story about chronicles, and it is very
old.

7
00:00:41,275 --> 00:00:44,244
Every chronicle was written by somebody.

8
00:00:44,244 --> 00:00:50,775
That somebody had a name, a patron, a city,
and a firm opinion about who had started it.

9
00:00:51,125 --> 00:00:58,800
The first surviving argument about how to write
history is also the first argument between two
historians.

10
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:03,859
Herodotus travelled, listened, and
wrote down what people told him,

11
00:01:03,859 --> 00:01:07,725
including the parts he plainly
did not believe himself.

12
00:01:08,075 --> 00:01:10,057
Thucydides did the opposite.

13
00:01:10,057 --> 00:01:17,700
He narrowed his subject to a single war, distrusted
hearsay, and wrote as though emotion were a
contaminant.

14
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:21,422
He never names Herodotus. He does not need to.

15
00:01:21,422 --> 00:01:26,700
The disdain is legible from a distance
of two and a half thousand years.

16
00:01:27,050 --> 00:01:33,150
He was also candid about one limit. The
speeches in his history are not transcripts.

17
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:40,175
He wrote what each speaker would probably have said,
keeping as near as he could to the general sense.

18
00:01:40,525 --> 00:01:43,360
That is an unusually honest admission,

19
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:50,225
and it means the founding document of rigorous
history contains dialogue its author made up.

20
00:01:50,575 --> 00:01:53,160
And it did not stop when they died.

21
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:58,700
Roughly five centuries later, Plutarch
wrote an entire essay attacking him.

22
00:01:59,050 --> 00:02:03,558
The title, in the manuscripts,
is On the Malice of Herodotus.

23
00:02:03,558 --> 00:02:09,100
Plutarch's central complaint is that
Herodotus had been rude about Boeotia.

24
00:02:09,450 --> 00:02:11,725
Plutarch was Boeotian.

25
00:02:12,075 --> 00:02:19,065
In the sixth century, Procopius of Caesarea
wrote the official account of Justinian's wars.

26
00:02:19,065 --> 00:02:22,675
It is admiring, detailed, and genuinely useful.

27
00:02:23,025 --> 00:02:28,055
He also wrote a second book, which
stayed unpublished while he lived.

28
00:02:28,055 --> 00:02:33,450
In that one, Justinian is a demon in
human form, and the empress is worse.

29
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:36,363
Same man. Same events. Same decade.

30
00:02:36,363 --> 00:02:42,075
The difference is that only one of the
two books could be read by the emperor.

31
00:02:42,425 --> 00:02:44,020
He was not lying twice.

32
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:50,400
He was writing for two audiences, and only one
of them was in a position to have him killed.

33
00:02:50,750 --> 00:02:54,745
Monastic chroniclers worked under a quieter pressure.

34
00:02:54,745 --> 00:03:00,700
Their houses held land, land was granted
by kings, and kings occasionally read.

35
00:03:01,050 --> 00:03:07,567
Bede, writing in Northumbria in the eighth
century, produced a history of the English church,

36
00:03:07,567 --> 00:03:11,000
and in the process largely assembled the English.

37
00:03:11,350 --> 00:03:16,452
There was no single English people
sitting there waiting to be described.

38
00:03:16,452 --> 00:03:20,575
There were rival kingdoms, and
a monk with a unifying idea.

39
00:03:20,925 --> 00:03:29,046
Two centuries later the Peterborough Chronicle reaches
the year 1137, lands in the middle of a civil war,

40
00:03:29,046 --> 00:03:31,675
and stops sounding administrative.

41
00:03:32,025 --> 00:03:35,698
It describes torture, famine, and burned villages.

42
00:03:35,698 --> 00:03:40,325
It says that men spoke openly
that Christ and his saints slept.

43
00:03:40,675 --> 00:03:46,907
Then it adds that all this was suffered for
our sins. The catastrophe was political.

44
00:03:46,907 --> 00:03:52,175
The explanation offered is theological.
That swap is itself a position.

45
00:03:52,525 --> 00:03:58,448
Around the same years, Geoffrey of Monmouth
published a history of the kings of Britain,

46
00:03:58,448 --> 00:04:01,275
running the line back to the fall of Troy.

47
00:04:01,625 --> 00:04:09,243
It handed Britain a royal descent older than Rome's,
and handed the Norman court a past worth inheriting.

48
00:04:09,243 --> 00:04:12,000
A great deal of it he simply invented.

49
00:04:12,350 --> 00:04:14,045
Some fifty years later,

50
00:04:14,045 --> 00:04:21,412
William of Newburgh opened his own history with a
prologue given over almost entirely to demolishing

51
00:04:21,412 --> 00:04:22,075
Geoffrey.

52
00:04:22,425 --> 00:04:30,210
Everything that man wrote, he said, was made up,
either by himself or by others. He objected to the
giants.

53
00:04:30,210 --> 00:04:32,175
He objected to the dragons.

54
00:04:32,525 --> 00:04:34,553
He also objected to the dates,

55
00:04:34,553 --> 00:04:39,825
which suggests he had read the whole
thing carefully before losing his temper.

56
00:04:40,175 --> 00:04:46,780
It is the earliest surviving case of a historian
beginning a book by attacking a dead colleague.

57
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:48,500
It would not be the last.

58
00:04:48,850 --> 00:04:55,970
In Kyiv, in roughly the same century, monks were
compiling what we now call the Primary Chronicle.

59
00:04:55,970 --> 00:04:59,675
It opens by asking where the land of Rus came from.

60
00:05:00,025 --> 00:05:05,699
Its answer is that the Slavic tribes,
unable to keep order among themselves,

61
00:05:05,699 --> 00:05:11,075
sent across the sea and invited
Varangian princes to come and rule them.

62
00:05:11,425 --> 00:05:15,075
Whether anything like that happened is still argued.

63
00:05:15,075 --> 00:05:22,025
What is not in doubt is how useful such a sentence
is to the dynasty already sitting on the throne.

64
00:05:22,375 --> 00:05:26,450
Six hundred years later, that passage detonated.

65
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:33,088
In 1749 Gerhard Friedrich Müller, a
German academician in Saint Petersburg,

66
00:05:33,088 --> 00:05:39,125
prepared an address deriving the very
name Rus from a Scandinavian root.

67
00:05:39,475 --> 00:05:46,900
Mikhail Lomonosov and others denounced it as
derogatory to Russia. The address was already printed.

68
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:49,000
The print run was destroyed.

69
00:05:49,350 --> 00:05:57,300
A line written by a monk in the twelfth century had
become, six centuries on, a question of state
security.

70
00:05:57,650 --> 00:06:02,601
For centuries the quarrel stayed
indoors, inside the church and the court.

71
00:06:02,601 --> 00:06:07,150
Printing moved it into the market,
where it got considerably louder.

72
00:06:07,500 --> 00:06:14,985
In 1776 Edward Gibbon published the first volume
of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire,

73
00:06:14,985 --> 00:06:19,250
with a charge buried in chapters fifteen and sixteen.

74
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:23,834
Rome, he suggested, had not
simply been overrun from outside.

75
00:06:23,834 --> 00:06:29,525
It had been hollowed out by a religion
that taught its citizens to look elsewhere.

76
00:06:29,875 --> 00:06:32,532
The clergy answered within the year.

77
00:06:32,532 --> 00:06:40,060
One reply appeared as An Apology for Christianity,
in a series of letters, addressed to Edward Gibbon,

78
00:06:40,060 --> 00:06:40,650
Esquire.

79
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,250
The argument now had a postal address.

80
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:45,771
Then, in 1824,

81
00:06:45,771 --> 00:06:53,800
a young German named Leopold von Ranke wrote a
preface that quietly rewrote the job description.

82
00:06:54,150 --> 00:07:01,395
History, he said, had been assigned the office of
judging the past. His own book did not aspire to that.

83
00:07:01,395 --> 00:07:04,600
It wanted only to show what actually happened.

84
00:07:04,950 --> 00:07:10,704
Out of that came the apparatus we still
use. Archives. Footnotes. The seminar.

85
00:07:10,704 --> 00:07:15,425
The demand that every claim carry
a source you can go and check.

86
00:07:15,775 --> 00:07:21,225
It was a real advance, and it is why
modern history is checkable at all.

87
00:07:21,575 --> 00:07:28,725
It was also built inside the Prussian state, staffed
by its graduates, and paid by its ministries.

88
00:07:28,725 --> 00:07:31,425
Its instincts were reliably national.

89
00:07:31,775 --> 00:07:36,460
The method was designed to remove
the historian from the account.

90
00:07:36,460 --> 00:07:39,775
What it actually removed was the visible ones.

91
00:07:40,125 --> 00:07:44,221
By the twentieth century that
had become the whole quarrel.

92
00:07:44,221 --> 00:07:49,775
If the historian cannot be subtracted,
what exactly is the thing being produced?

93
00:07:50,125 --> 00:07:54,729
In 1931 Herbert Butterfield
gave the problem a usable name.

94
00:07:54,729 --> 00:08:01,675
Whig history: reading the past as a run-up to
the present, with the present as the point.

95
00:08:02,025 --> 00:08:08,752
Every generation that finds its own arrangements
foreshadowed in the twelfth century is doing it.

96
00:08:08,752 --> 00:08:14,300
Naming it did not stop anyone. It made
it visible, which is a different service.

97
00:08:14,650 --> 00:08:16,872
Two years earlier, in Strasbourg,

98
00:08:16,872 --> 00:08:23,200
Marc Bloch and Lucien Febvre had founded a journal
to attack the same problem from underneath.

99
00:08:23,550 --> 00:08:27,472
Kings and battles, they argued, were surface weather.

100
00:08:27,472 --> 00:08:34,650
Below them sat grain prices, roads, disease,
climate, and what ordinary people actually believed.

101
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:39,136
Fernand Braudel later called
that layer the longue durée:

102
00:08:39,136 --> 00:08:44,650
the history that moves so slowly nobody
living inside it can feel it moving.

103
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:50,004
A failed Mediterranean harvest
reshapes more lives than a coronation.

104
00:08:50,004 --> 00:08:56,675
It is simply harder to put in a chronicle,
because it does not happen on any particular day.

105
00:08:57,025 --> 00:09:05,225
Marc Bloch joined the French Resistance. He was
captured, tortured, and shot by the Gestapo in 1944.

106
00:09:05,575 --> 00:09:11,073
He left behind a short unfinished book
asking what history is actually for,

107
00:09:11,073 --> 00:09:14,225
and what the historian's craft consists of.

108
00:09:14,575 --> 00:09:20,919
In 1961 E. H. Carr compressed a century
of suspicion into a single instruction.

109
00:09:20,919 --> 00:09:26,300
Study the historian, he wrote,
before you begin to study the facts.

110
00:09:26,650 --> 00:09:31,267
That sounds like cynicism. It is
closer to a maintenance procedure.

111
00:09:31,267 --> 00:09:37,675
Nobody is telling you the account is worthless.
You are being told to check who assembled it.

112
00:09:38,025 --> 00:09:42,425
Which brings us to the genuinely
strange thing about the present.

113
00:09:42,775 --> 00:09:46,543
For almost all of this story, editing was invisible.

114
00:09:46,543 --> 00:09:52,050
A monk revised a chronicle, and the
earlier version simply stopped existing.

115
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:58,189
A publisher mailed out replacement pages,
and the old page went into the fire.

116
00:09:58,189 --> 00:10:01,900
The record changed, and the change left no record.

117
00:10:02,250 --> 00:10:09,400
That is what has actually broken, and it is worth
being precise about which part. Not the politics.

118
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:11,350
The politics is a constant.

119
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:13,525
The concealment.

120
00:10:13,875 --> 00:10:18,976
An encyclopedia now keeps every
version of itself it has ever had.

121
00:10:18,976 --> 00:10:23,150
Every edit is timestamped, attributed, and reversible.

122
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:28,875
The arguments happen on a page of
their own, and that page is saved too.

123
00:10:29,225 --> 00:10:35,450
You can watch a dispute over a single
sentence run for years and read every round.

124
00:10:35,800 --> 00:10:40,775
The fight over the record is not new.
The fight being on the record is.

125
00:10:41,125 --> 00:10:43,825
None of that makes an account neutral.

126
00:10:43,825 --> 00:10:49,225
Somebody still decides what deserves
an article and what is merely a detail.

127
00:10:49,575 --> 00:10:53,587
But the deciding is now written
down beside the decision.

128
00:10:53,587 --> 00:10:57,950
The chronicle has acquired a
margin, and the margin is public.

129
00:10:58,300 --> 00:11:04,150
Beria was removed with a razor blade,
and there is no way left to see the cut.

130
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:09,600
Today it would be one grey line in
a log, with a timestamp, and a name.
